/*
 * apt-conf-hook-dropped — a captured apt.conf.d command hook must never run
 * on the builder.
 *
 * Protects: the arbitrary-code-execution finding in
 * docs/security/review-2026-09.md, and core/apt/aptconf.go's allow-list
 * (filterAptConf). apt runs APT::Update::Pre-Invoke through /bin/sh on every
 * `apt-get update`, as root, and apt-get still exits 0 afterwards — so a
 * hook that fires leaves no trace in any exit code, warning or resolved
 * plan. A snapshot is untrusted input carried across the air gap, and the
 * builder is the one host in this design holding a signing key, so a hook
 * the builder honours is remote code execution on exactly the machine that
 * must not have any.
 *
 * Why this fixture exists at all, when core/apt already unit-tests the
 * filter: the unit tests prove filterAptConf drops the key from a byte
 * slice. They cannot prove that the file apt ACTUALLY reads is the filtered
 * one — that is a property of how the private root is assembled and how
 * APT_CONFIG is set (docs/experiments/E4-*.md: `-o Dir::Etc::parts=` cannot
 * carry the target's apt.conf.d, because apt reads that directory before
 * command-line -o processing, so only the environment variable works). The
 * only honest evidence that a command never ran is that the file it would
 * have created is not there.
 *
 * How it is constructed, and why each half is needed:
 *
 *   - The target's apt.conf.d gets APT::Update::Pre-Invoke, whose payload
 *     touches a marker in /tmp. ApplyTargetState writes apt.conf.d overrides
 *     before its own `apt-get update` (test/e2e/harness/target.go), so the
 *     hook fires there — and the "state" container's present:true check
 *     asserts exactly that. That check is about the FIXTURE, not the
 *     product: it proves the payload this row plants really does execute
 *     when something honours it. Without it, a mistyped path or a hook apt
 *     never invokes would leave the negative check below passing for a
 *     reason having nothing to do with debark — the vacuous-test trap this
 *     harness has already been bitten by five times.
 *   - The "builder" container's present:false check is the product claim:
 *     the same captured hook, carried in the snapshot and assembled into the
 *     private root, must not have executed while debark resolved.
 *
 * The build is required to SUCCEED, and that requirement is load-bearing
 * rather than incidental: a successful build means apt-get update genuinely
 * ran inside the private root against the captured configuration. Marker
 * absent alone would also be satisfied by a build that fell over before apt
 * ever started, which would prove nothing. Success plus absence is what
 * pins the claim down.
 *
 * The lock.json assertion is deliberately secondary. A warning proves only
 * that the filter NOTICED the key; the absent marker proves it worked. Both
 * are checked because the warning is also the operator-facing mitigation for
 * every setting the allow-list withholds — a fidelity loss nobody is told
 * about is its own defect — but if only one of the two could be kept, it
 * would be the marker.
 *
 * What would break if this regressed: any target whose apt.conf.d a third
 * party can write — a compromised machine, a shared appliance image, a
 * snapshot mailed in by the very customer the bundle is being built for —
 * would execute its chosen command as root on the build host, and nothing
 * in the build output would say so.
 */
{
  "schema_version": "debark.e2e.fixture/v1",
  "name": "apt-conf-hook-dropped",
  "protects": "docs/security/review-2026-09.md RCE via captured apt.conf.d; core/apt/aptconf.go filterAptConf allow-list.1 apt.conf.d capture",
  "tags": ["edge", "security"],
  "target": {
    "distro": "debian",
    "version": "12",
    "arch": "amd64",
    "apt_conf": {
      "APT::Update::Pre-Invoke": "{\"touch /tmp/debark-apt-conf-hook-fired\";}"
    },
    "installed": {}
  },
  "request": {
    "packages": ["jq"]
  },
  "expect": {
    "build": { "exit_class": "success" },
    "packages_present": ["jq"],
    "container_paths": [
      { "container": "state", "path": "/tmp/debark-apt-conf-hook-fired", "present": true },
      { "container": "builder", "path": "/tmp/debark-apt-conf-hook-fired", "present": false }
    ],
    "bundle_files": [
      { "path": "lock.json", "contains": ["private-root.apt-conf-dropped", "APT::Update::Pre-Invoke"] }
    ]
  }
}
